#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# fourier_coefficient

#% text
# `fourier_coefficient(f, t, n)` gives the `n`-th coefficient of the exponential Fourier series of `f`, `1/(2 pi)` times the integral of `f exp(-im n t)` for `t` from `-pi` to `pi`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# With `fourier_parameters: [a, b]` the coefficient is `(abs(b)/(2 pi))^((1 + a)/2)` times the integral of `f exp(-im b n t)` over `[-pi/abs(b), pi/abs(b)]`; the default is `[1, 1]`.

#% item
# For a symbolic `n` (an integer) the result is the generic coefficient; the indices where its formula has a pole come as separate cases of a `piecewise`, and a single nonzero index as a `discrete_delta`.

#% item
# `fourier_coefficient(f, [t1, t2, ...], [n1, n2, ...])` gives a coefficient of a function of several variables.

#% item
# `assumptions: c` is used in simplifying, for instance `element(a, integers)` for a symbolic frequency.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
fourier_coefficient(t^2 + t, t, 5)
#% output
#= -2/25 + -im/5

#% cell
fourier_coefficient(t^2 + t, t, n)
#% output
#= piecewise([[(1/3)*pi^2, n == 0]], (2 + im*n)*(-1)^n/n^2)

#% cell
fourier_coefficient(abs(t), t, n)
#% output
#= piecewise([[(1/2)*pi, n == 0]], (-1 + (-1)^n)/(n^2*pi))

#% cell
fourier_coefficient(exp(3*im*t), t, n)
#% output
#= discrete_delta(-3 + n)

#% cell
fourier_coefficient(t^3, t, n, fourier_parameters: [1, -2*pi])
#% output
#= piecewise([[0, n == 0]], (3*im/4 - (im/8)*n^2*pi^2)*(-1)^n/(n^3*pi^3))

#% cell
fourier_coefficient(exp(im*a*t), t, n, assumptions: element(a, integers))
#% output
#= discrete_delta(n - a)

#% section
# See also

#% text
